          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Universal bundles – or
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cocylinder"} mapping cocylinder
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fibers"} homotopy fibers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
            are intermediate steps in the computation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+cofibers"} homotopy cofibers
          %p
            It is familiar from topology that one can form the path fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P X \to X
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            – or, if things are not
            = succeed "al," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+objects"} comma objects
          %p
            Since universal bundles are examples of this construction, we here speak of
            = succeed "." do
              %em generalized universal bundles
            = succeed "." do
              %em generalized path fibrations
          %p
            One generalizaton of “generalized universal bundles” is that the objects in question need not be groupoidal, i.e. they behave like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+spaces"} directed spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
            familiar from topology are replaced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+object"} comma object
            constructions. This is useful in various applications. For instance the constructions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} category of elements
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
            can be understood as such directed homotopy pullbacks of the point.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pt
                        %mi B
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt \stackrel{pt_B}{\to}B
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the
            %strong
              generalized universal
              = succeed "-bundle" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is (if it exists) the morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi pt
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p : \mathbf{E}_{pt} B \to B
          %p
            which is the total composite vertical morphism of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} pt
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi pt
                                  %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi I
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi d
                                  %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{E}_{\mathrm{pt}}B
                  \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                  pt
                  \\\
                  \\big\downarrow
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\big\downarrow{}^{\mathrlap{pt_B}}
                  \\\
                  [I,B]
                  \&amp;\stackrel{d_1}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                  B
                  \\\
                  \\big\downarrow{}^{\mathrlap{d_0}}
                  \\\
                  B
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            So the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi pt
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}_{pt}B := [I,B]\times_{B} pt
            is defined to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of the diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 1
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pt
                        %mi B
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,B] \stackrel{d_1}{\to} B \stackrel{pt_B}{\leftarrow} pt
            and the morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi pt
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}_{pt}B \to B
            is the composite of the left vertical morphism in the above diagram which comes from the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0
          %p
            Then a (generalized) “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            \-bundle” on some object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of the generalized universal
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}_{pt}
            along a “classifying morphism”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : X \to B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
